---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  TrappingWater,
  DEFAULT_HEIGHTS,
} from "../../components/cave/trapping-rain-water";
import RevealCode from "../../components/RevealCode";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="Trapping Rain Water"
  description="Two-pointer algorithm visualization with pixel art sprites"
  theme="dark"
>
  <!-- Intro: the terrain -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      Look at this terrain. Hills and valleys. A quiet landscape. But something
      is about to happen.
    </aside>
    <TrappingWater
      client:load
      heights={DEFAULT_HEIGHTS}
      showAlgorithm={false}
      showWaterPreview={false}
      showControls={false}
      showRain={false}
      scale={0.6}
      width={1080}
      height={720}
    />
  </section>

  <!-- It rains -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      It rains. Water falls from the sky, and the valleys start to fill. Some of
      it flows away. But some of it stays, trapped between the walls.
    </aside>
    <TrappingWater
      client:load
      heights={DEFAULT_HEIGHTS}
      showAlgorithm={false}
      showWaterPreview={false}
      showControls={false}
      showRain={true}
      scale={0.6}
      width={1080}
      height={720}
    />
  </section>

  <!-- Water trapped -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      The question is: how much water gets trapped? We know the shape of the
      terrain. We know where the hills are, and where the gaps are. Can we
      figure out the answer without simulating every single raindrop?
    </aside>
    <TrappingWater
      client:load
      heights={DEFAULT_HEIGHTS}
      showAlgorithm={false}
      showWaterPreview={true}
      showControls={false}
      showRain={true}
      scale={0.6}
      width={1080}
      height={720}
    />
  </section>

  <!-- The problem statement -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      Let's make it formal. We receive a list of numbers. Each number represents
      the height of a column. Our job is to figure out how many units of water
      would be trapped between them after it rains. Sounds simple, right? Let's
      start small.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <div class="text-2xl text-gray-300 space-y-4 text-center max-w-3xl">
        <p class="fragment">
          Given the height of each <span class="text-amber-400"
            >terrain column</span
          >...
        </p>
        <p class="fragment">
          Calculate how many <span class="text-blue-400">water blocks</span> get trapped.
        </p>
        <p class="fragment fade-up text-amber-300 font-mono mt-8">
          heights = [0, 1, 0, 2, 1, 0, 1, 3, 2, 1, 2, 1]
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Intro example: single column -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      Let's think about the simplest case. A single column. It rains. Where
      would the water go? There is nothing to hold it. No walls on either side.
      It just falls off. Zero water trapped.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-10">
      <code class="text-3xl tracking-wider text-amber-300">[1]</code>
      <TrappingWater
        client:load
        heights={[0]}
        showAlgorithm={false}
        showWaterPreview={true}
        showControls={false}
        showRain={true}
        scale={0.7}
      />
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 fragment">Nothing to hold it. Zero.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Intro example: two columns -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      Two columns now. A short one and a tall one. Still, no gap between them.
      No valley where the water could rest. It slides down the sides and
      disappears. Still zero.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-10">
      <code class="text-3xl tracking-wider text-amber-300">[0, 1]</code>
      <TrappingWater
        client:load
        heights={[0, 1]}
        showAlgorithm={false}
        showWaterPreview={true}
        showControls={false}
        showRain={true}
        scale={0.7}
      />
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 fragment">No valley. Still zero.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Intro example: 1, 0, 1 -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now this is interesting. A wall, a gap, and another wall. Both walls are
        one block tall. The gap between them can hold exactly one unit of water.
        And it does.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the smallest meaningful case. Two walls and a valley between
        them.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-10">
      <code class="text-3xl tracking-wider text-amber-300">[1, 0, 1]</code>
      <TrappingWater
        client:load
        heights={[1, 0, 1]}
        showAlgorithm={false}
        showWaterPreview={true}
        showControls={false}
        showRain={true}
        scale={0.7}
      />
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 fragment">
        Two walls, one gap. <span class="text-blue-400">One unit</span> of water.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Intro example: 1, 0, 2 — the key insight -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        But what if the walls are not the same height? One on the left, two on
        the right. The right wall is taller. Could we fill two blocks of water?
      </p>
      <p>
        No. The water can only rise as high as the shorter wall. Think about it.
        If you pour water into this gap, it overflows on the left side at height
        one. The right wall being taller changes nothing. The bottleneck is the
        shorter side.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the water at any position is limited by the minimum of the tallest
        wall on each side. This is the key insight.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-10">
      <code class="text-3xl tracking-wider text-amber-300">[1, 0, 2]</code>
      <TrappingWater
        client:load
        heights={[1, 0, 2]}
        showAlgorithm={false}
        showWaterPreview={true}
        showControls={false}
        showRain={true}
        scale={0.7}
      />
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 fragment">
        Still <span class="text-blue-400">one unit</span>. The water can only
        rise to the <span class="text-amber-400">shorter wall</span>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes"></aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <code class="text-3xl tracking-wider text-amber-300">[1, 0, 2]</code>
      <TrappingWater
        client:load
        heights={[2, 1, 0, 1, 2]}
        showAlgorithm={false}
        showWaterPreview={true}
        showControls={false}
        showRain={true}
        scale={0.7}
      />
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 fragment">
        Still <span class="text-blue-400">one unit</span>. The water can only
        rise to the <span class="text-amber-400">shorter wall</span>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <!-- Two pointers idea -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        So how do we solve this for a larger terrain? We could check every
        single column, find the tallest wall to its left, the tallest to its
        right, take the minimum, and subtract the column's own height. That
        gives us the water at that position.
      </p>
      <p>
        But there is a more elegant way. Imagine two people standing at opposite
        ends of the valley. One on the left, one on the right. They start
        walking toward each other.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each one remembers the tallest wall they have seen so far. That memory
        is what tells them how much water could be held at their current
        position.
      </p>
      <p>
        The one standing behind the shorter wall moves first. Why? Because the
        shorter side is the bottleneck. We already saw this with our small
        example. Water can only be held up to the height of the shorter wall. So
        we always know exactly how much water fits there.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-4">
      <TrappingWater
        client:load
        heights={DEFAULT_HEIGHTS}
        showAlgorithm={true}
        showWaterPreview={false}
        showControls={true}
        autoPlay={false}
        autoPlayDelay={600}
        scale={0.375}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Auto-play -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's spell out the rules. Each pointer keeps track of the tallest
        column it has seen. That is its maximum. When a pointer steps forward,
        it looks down. If the ground is lower than its maximum, the difference
        is water. Simple subtraction.
      </p>
      <p>
        And the key question at every step: who moves? The one behind the
        shorter wall. Always. Because we are certain about how much water fits
        on the shorter side. The taller side might still grow.
      </p>
      <p>
        They keep walking. One step at a time. Until they meet in the middle.
        Let's watch it play out.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-4">
      <TrappingWater
        client:load
        heights={DEFAULT_HEIGHTS}
        showAlgorithm={true}
        showWaterPreview={false}
        showControls={true}
        autoPlay={true}
        autoPlayDelay={500}
        scale={0.375}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Different terrain -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      What about a different landscape? Taller peaks, deeper valleys. The
      algorithm doesn't care. The same two pointers. The same simple rule. It
      works regardless of the shape. That's the beauty of it.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-4">
      <TrappingWater
        client:load
        heights={[3, 0, 2, 0, 4, 0, 1, 0, 3, 0, 2]}
        showAlgorithm={true}
        showWaterPreview={false}
        showControls={true}
        autoPlay={false}
        scale={0.3125}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Interactive -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      Now it's your turn. Change the heights. Build your own terrain. Step
      through the algorithm and watch how the water fills in. Try making a deep
      valley. Try a single tall peak. Try something flat. See what happens.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-4">
      <TrappingWater
        client:load
        heights={DEFAULT_HEIGHTS}
        showAlgorithm={true}
        showWaterPreview={false}
        showControls={true}
        showHeightEditor={true}
        autoPlay={false}
        scale={0.375}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Code -->
  <section id="code" class="text-lg">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's see the code. We start with two pointers, one at each end. We also
        keep track of the tallest wall each pointer has seen. And a counter for
        the total water.
      </p>
      <p>
        At each step, we compare the heights at both pointers. The side with the
        shorter column moves inward. If the column is shorter than the tallest
        wall on that side, we add the difference as water. If it is taller, we
        update the maximum.
      </p>
      <p>
        We repeat until the pointers meet. One pass. No extra memory. Linear
        time, constant space. Elegant.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def trap(height):
    if not height:
        return 0

    # STEP: Two pointers, one at each end
    left, right = 0, len(height) - 1
    # STEP: Each remembers the tallest wall it has seen
    left_max = right_max = 0
    water = 0

    # STEP: Walk toward each other
    while left < right:
        # STEP: The shorter side moves
        if height[left] <= height[right]:
            # STEP: New wall? Update max. Otherwise, add water.
            if height[left] >= left_max:
                left_max = height[left]
            else:
                water += left_max - height[left]
            left += 1
        else:
            if height[right] >= right_max:
                right_max = height[right]
            else:
                water += right_max - height[right]
            right -= 1

    # STEP: Done. One pass, constant space.
    return water`}
    />
  </section>

  <!-- Closing -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      This was today's Cheese Byte. A landscape of numbers, a storm of rain, and
      two travelers walking toward each other, measuring puddles as they go.
      Sometimes the simplest walk reveals the deepest answer.
    </aside>
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
